                    "time": "2023-03-08T12:14:00-05:00"
                  },
                  "committer": {
                    "name": "Drew DeVault",
                    "email": "redacted@example.invalid",
                    "time": "2023-03-13T12:53:39+01:00"
                  },
                  "message": "get rid of some unused includes\n\nIn main.c, unistd.h is a fairly big stick to use just to get stdint\ntypes, so downgrade that header.\n",
                  "parents": [
                    {
                      "id": "d562d7efb85fc4a498eedc6b1abc8a8e4443e1f4"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": "d562d7efb85fc4a498eedc6b1abc8a8e4443e1f4",
                  "shortId": "d562d7e",
                  "author": {
                    "name": "Eli Schwartz",
                    "email": "redacted@example.invalid",
                    "time": "2023-03-08T11:56:09-05:00"
                  },
                  "committer": {
                    "name": "Drew DeVault",
                    "email": "redacted@example.invalid",
                    "time": "2023-03-13T12:53:30+01:00"
                  },
                  "message": "remove useless re-declarations of standard functions\n\nHistorically, scdoc has used a project-local string.h, which overrode\nand masked <string.h> and caused issues.\n\nFor example, strstr and strerror were impossible to obtain definitions\nfor. Instead of fixing the clashing names, the definitions were locally\ncopied into main.c\n\nIn commit 309ff4672b7f5cba9bca1ce8657f767b51f32220 this was fixed\nproperly (by renaming string.h to str.h) as part of adding printf usage\nto the file as well. But the old hacks were never cleaned up.\n",
                  "parents": [
                    {
                      "id": "1923fb61cd71182f05d029f280d056b21f66e8a0"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": "1923fb61cd71182f05d029f280d056b21f66e8a0",
                  "shortId": "1923fb6",
                  "author": {
                    "name": "Dimitry Andric",
                    "email": "redacted@example.invalid",
                    "time": "2022-12-09T17:59:32+01:00"
                  },
                  "committer": {
                    "name": "Drew DeVault",
                    "email": "redacted@example.invalid",
                    "time": "2022-12-19T12:01:40+01:00"
                  },
                  "message": "Fix clang 15 -Wstrict-prototypes warnings\n\nAs of https://github.com/llvm/llvm-project/commit/11da1b53d8c, clang 15\nhas started warning about functions which take no arguments, but are\ndeclared or defined using \"()\" instead of \"(void)\". See also [1].\n\nAt first this was even an error by default, but before clang 15 was\nreleased, it was again downgraded to a warning. Since scdoc builds\nwith both \"-std=c99 -pedantic\" and \"-Wall -Wextra -Werror\", this leads\nto two compile errors:\n\n    include/str.h:10:23: error: a function declaration without a prototype is deprecated in all versions of C [-Werror,-Wstrict-prototypes]\n    struct str *str_create();\n                          ^\n                           void\n    src/string.c:15:23: error: a function declaration without a prototype is deprecated in all versions of C [-Werror,-Wstrict-prototypes]\n    struct str *str_create() {\n                          ^\n                           void\n\nTo fix this, use \"(void)\" for both the declaration and definition of the\nstr_create function.\n\n[1] https://discourse.llvm.org/t/rfc-enabling-wstrict-prototypes-by-default-in-c/60521\n",
                  "parents": [
                    {
                      "id": "4af1e1e3a8ebfddcc11a189254d60fc4ad74cd59"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": "4af1e1e3a8ebfddcc11a189254d60fc4ad74cd59",
                  "shortId": "4af1e1e",
                  "author": {
                    "name": "Humm",
                    "email": "redacted@example.invalid",
                    "time": "2022-01-14T00:56:18Z"
                  },
                  "committer": {
                    "name": "Drew DeVault",
                    "email": "redacted@example.invalid",
                    "time": "2022-01-14T08:47:25+01:00"
                  },
                  "message": "simplify list output\n\nFor lists, plain old indented paragraphs are the perfect tool.  To not\nhave vertical space between list items, we use .PD 0.\n\nShould the many needless .PP be removed, will the .PD 0 have to be moved\nafter the first list item, such that that introduces vertical space\nafter the last paragraph.\n",
                  "parents": [
                    {
                      "id": "b16de454afc736a87c0386390c4cc91ba3cc8f29"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": "b16de454afc736a87c0386390c4cc91ba3cc8f29",
                  "shortId": "b16de45",
                  "author": {
                    "name": "Humm",
                    "email": "redacted@example.invalid",
                    "time": "2022-01-02T01:54:26Z"
